"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Location"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/venues/688"} "Palau Sant Jordi, Barcelona"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/70663"} "92 from 24 countries"]]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "The format was the now standard of 36 gymnasts advancing to a final round from the team all-around, with no more than three gymnasts allowed per nation. For the first time the final was independent of the qualifying round, as the qualifying scores were not carried over. The favorites were the last two World Champions, Belarussian " [:a {:href "/athletes/28117"} "Svetlana Boginskaya"] " (1989), the Olympic bronze medalist in 1988, and American " [:a {:href "/athletes/29302"} "Kim Zmeskal"] " (1991), and the recent European Champion, " [:a {:href "/athletes/29187"} "Tetiana Hutsu"] ". However, Hutsu placed ninth in team qualifying, and as only the fourth Unified Team member, did not seem to make the final. Conveniently for her, her teammate, " [:a {:href "/athletes/29053"} "Roza Galiyeva"] ", was reported by Unified officials to have aggravated a knee injury and had to withdraw from the final, clearing the way for Hutsu."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The final came down to the last rotation. Zmeskal was out of it and would finish 10th, but Boginskaya and Hutsu were challenged by American " [:a {:href "/athletes/29266"} "Shannon Miller"] " and Romanian " [:a {:href "/athletes/29022"} "Lavinia Miloşovici"] ". Miller and Hutsu were on the vault, and Hutsu’s final vault of 9.95 brought her the gold medal, with Miller second and Miloşovici receiving the bronze medal. Hutsu was given a bonus of $3,000 from the Unified Team, half of which she gave to Roza Galiyeva."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Number"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "Points"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {:class "bib"} "242"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/29187"} 